WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.832 --> 00:00:06.135
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.135 --> 00:00:08.010
MARIE VENDETTUOLI: Hi,
I'm Marie Vendettuoli.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.370
I'm a senior statistical
programmer at Fred Hutch

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.800
and the Statistical Center
for HIV/AIDS Research

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:13.880
and Prevention.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.940
At RStudio Conf 2020,
my colleague Ellis Hughes

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.800
shared technical
details demonstrating

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.470
how R organization is tackling
the challenge of software

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.690
validation for R packages.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.160
It's now a year later.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:26.840
We have three packages
released and are

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.790
tackling the extended
challenge of ensuring

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:32.083
that our entire verse is fit for
purpose as an integrated unit.

00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:33.500
Today, I'll be
sharing my thoughts

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.630
regarding multi package
development in the pharma

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:36.740
environment.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.750
My first recommendation
is to set the team up

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.570
for success by getting
infrastructure in order.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.910
Moving away from historical
validation practices

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.090
needs updating or
generating new SOPs.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.890
Another area for
infrastructure support

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.320
is to establish common
development guidelines that

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.150
get shared across all packages.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.070
This is especially
important if packages

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.790
are implementing legacy code
that was previously siloed.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.670
The ultimate benefit, however,
is code portability within

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:02.930
the overall project.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.510
My next recommendation is the
need to address development

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:06.950
from an empathy perspective.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.830
At Scharp, we observe
four distinct roles, one,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.780
users rapidly processing data.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.900
These would be primarily
statistical programmers.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.840
Two, data analyst
reviewing code for context.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.340
These would be
individuals who need

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.650
to be able to rapidly
assess context

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.300
from function names law.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.000
Three, stakeholders interacting
with function solely

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.800
from the package
validation report.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.180
So this could be
someone involved

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.460
in writing specifications
and test cases

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.120
but aren't participating
in coding activity.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.670
Four, package developers
and maintainers

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.520
who seek to eliminate
duplication or re-implementation

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.960
with package updates.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.600
Now one common need that transcends
all of these user groups

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.180
is to have a vocabulary, which
allows us to place functions

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.180
within the larger context of
assay specific data processing

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.060
tasks, that is, we want
to survey what has been

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.120
implemented, filter
either by assay or task,

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.280
and call up help page
documentation on demand.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.090
So this is actually a reference
to Schneiderman's information

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.310
seeking mantra, which
is typically cited

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.264
in visualizations literature.

00:02:06.264 --> 00:02:08.389
In this context, we are
treating the function names

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:10.340
as identifying
handle and leveraging

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.310
RStudio's autocomplete as
the filtering mechanism

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:17.120
as we navigate to a particular
help page or call the function.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.250
For example, one step
of data processing

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.500
that occurs for all assays
is the transformation

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.290
to a cdisc ADaM
Basic Data Structure.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.870
Because of the function naming
convention we have chosen,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.870
a list of all available BDS
transformation functions

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.900
are displayed and we can select
for the assay of interest.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.250
Focusing on the package
validation elements,

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.890
we also need a
systematic approach

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.560
to how we map package
requirements, test cases,

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.300
and test code files.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.150
Validation as a form of user
acceptance testing, where

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.430
we have to show that for
every software requirement

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.220
there was an appropriate
test successfully executed.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.870
The modular approach
we use at Scharp

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.270
is incredibly flexible,
which has the development

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:54.740
benefits I will discuss later.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.800
But it does require some
organization to track.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.230
In addition to enforcing
a file naming convention,

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.860
we also scrape these files
to generate a tabular display

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.710
in the validation report, which
links requirements and tests.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.360
Between these two
implementations,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.150
both developers and
validation report stakeholders

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:14.960
are able to verify at a glance
that complete coverage exists.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.460
The last couple
of recommendations

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.980
I have addressed the
topic of technical debt.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.710
You may be wondering, why
are we splitting assays

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.330
into separate packages?

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:24.600
A couple of reasons.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.280
First, FTA guidance ask us to
revalidate the entire system

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.900
one element changes.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.750
Now change is expected,
input data structures

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.270
get updated,
analysis needs drift.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.490
However, an update
to one assay type

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.620
should not introduce
the appearance of change

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:41.450
affecting other assays.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.520
Secondly, from the
user perspective,

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.860
we need to put some conceptual
structures around processes.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.520
Sure, every data set
will be converted to BDS

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.950
but the definition of
that basic data structure

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:53.960
will vary from assay to assay.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.240
We need to capture that
distinction in a manner that

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:57.770
is easy to digest.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.310
What we also want to avoid is
excessive re-implementation.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.310
As we have expanded to
multiple assay packages,

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.140
we have identified
utility functions

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.510
that could be shared and moved
into their own foundation

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:09.380
package.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.480
We can enforce package
code dependencies

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.120
through routine use of
the description file.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.100
With the power of
modular validation

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.100
means that we can move the
associated specifications,

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.740
test cases, and test
code files as well.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:25.160
Validation of the lighter assay
package with the new utility

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.400
package is simply compiling
the R Markdown document

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.740
without any need
to rewrite content.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.070
Likewise, when we need to add
additional specifications, test

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.680
cases, or test code to
an existing package,

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.760
an updated validation report
will add new child documents

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.440
to existing sources.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.120
This has the added
benefit of being

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.750
able to use a simple dev to
compare validation reports

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.250
across package versions.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.930
So I wanted to take
a moment to thank

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.530
those who made
this work possible,

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.680
especially the preceding
efforts by Ellis Hughes

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.590
and the combined contribution of
the Scharp Data Standardization

00:04:58.590 --> 00:04:59.400
team.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.460
Future work includes formalizing
our validation practice

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.260
through a fuse or
a collaboration

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.850
with deliverables
expected later in 2021.

